{:edb/db-before-t 2184 :edb/db-after-t 2185 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535497 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:15:21.592Z" 13194139535497 true] [17592186133815 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352174 13194139535497 true] [17592186133815 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352181 13194139535497 true] [17592186133816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352160 13194139535497 true] [17592186133816 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352167 13194139535497 true] [17592186134006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186130599 13194139535497 false] [17592186134006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352154 13194139535497 true] [17592186134006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535497 true] [17592186134006 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066119/assets/8417d2d62e9213ec3f92108495107fc982dc399e52cca8982e01516457f5692a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." 13194139535497 true] [17592186134006 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2109/Source/Images/q-124123.png)\n\nEvaluate $∫_{C}\\frac{2y}{x}dy$, where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse $9x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 36$, traversed from the point $(2, 0)$ to the point $(0, 3)$, as shown above." 13194139535497 false] [17592186134006 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352155 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352156 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352157 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352158 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352159 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1041 "selection" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352156 13194139535497 true] [17592186352154 1110 #uuid "5eb72313-8ba5-569d-bd76-f60035904f2e" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352155 1034 "\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352155 1111 #uuid "cfed41a4-f985-5d8f-8bfb-15549df2c0ad" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352156 1034 "9" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352156 1111 #uuid "62ad18bc-ddd8-5617-b9b1-cac8771819e6" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352157 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352157 1111 #uuid "265c4427-60a3-5842-ad27-36656c04e5ba" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352158 1034 "0" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352158 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352158 1111 #uuid "593cf411-d24f-5759-aa47-52740577e2b6" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352159 1034 "\\frac{9}{4}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352159 1111 #uuid "d35edd41-358e-5c9e-853d-1093a96f8a98" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352160 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352161 13194139535497 true] [17592186352160 1089 "q-124175" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352160 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535497 true] [17592186352160 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066119/assets/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(\\sqrt{x-1}+y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." 13194139535497 true] [17592186352160 1113 #uuid "7e7fe208-545d-517b-a221-dc0140c73316" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352160 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352162 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352163 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352164 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352165 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352166 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1041 "selection" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352162 13194139535497 true] [17592186352161 1110 #uuid "7af62dda-d68c-5c00-bb57-3f8bec4f58e4" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352162 1034 "\\frac{13}{6}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352162 1111 #uuid "10bf22ff-1d37-5615-a0d2-3b6919650d58" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352163 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352163 1111 #uuid "d8854a1a-391d-581d-9781-7be91bc86ebd" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352164 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352164 1111 #uuid "174d65c4-9616-5be0-9eb4-1d38c85da8ca" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352165 1034 "\\frac{10}{3}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352165 1111 #uuid "81ff744f-5e4e-5050-874f-7a16e3f2eb69" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352166 1034 "\\frac{15}{4}" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352166 1111 #uuid "d70814cc-b73e-5e5a-b2d7-ce208dfea8ed" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352167 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352168 13194139535497 true] [17592186352167 1089 "q-124177" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352167 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535497 true] [17592186352167 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066119/assets/272b0afabca579f65cbaa7949fd1be70427ff0fd860eb953207bbc8eda7b6e9c.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{3x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-left quarter of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0),$ as shown above." 13194139535497 true] [17592186352167 1113 #uuid "4a81f809-64bd-5578-8059-0ccd53e29b69" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352167 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352169 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352170 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352171 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352172 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352173 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1041 "selection" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352170 13194139535497 true] [17592186352168 1110 #uuid "917323fc-71c2-5089-8ea2-2a988a2dcb16" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352169 1034 "12" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352169 1111 #uuid "23187d9f-9f5f-58d0-af79-ea3d7b022e40" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352170 1034 "9" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352170 1111 #uuid "f8c54783-6256-59c1-a737-4ed96f0d6223" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352171 1034 "3" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352171 1111 #uuid "426a5f6d-092e-56b4-9231-cae40de78bc1" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352172 1034 "-18" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352172 1111 #uuid "26c8ffe3-42c4-59aa-9857-153eae63aa45" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352173 1034 "-6" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352173 1111 #uuid "459e44d2-36bd-5a8b-9b8b-b0930d6174d5" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352174 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352175 13194139535497 true] [17592186352174 1089 "q-124277" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352174 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535497 true] [17592186352174 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066119/assets/f532544febd248846eede65941f4617d325d0f91fec291d2679ba28abb2fb8fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352174 1113 #uuid "48afea1b-7419-5b27-97ac-ca8861895f05" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352174 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352176 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352177 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352178 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352179 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352180 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1041 "selection" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352176 13194139535497 true] [17592186352175 1110 #uuid "e4677ee8-4e7a-5d3f-ae70-1441a85109bf" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352176 1034 "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352176 1111 #uuid "04e05201-ffe4-5f35-aac1-9d6a5ba42ea1" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352177 1034 "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352177 1111 #uuid "939cf0eb-ec19-5fb5-ab90-1bb83ecba1c4" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352178 1034 "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352178 1111 #uuid "13ba3a31-419e-55d6-92d7-e99b3f85e7e8" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352179 1034 "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352179 1111 #uuid "6f192825-2bb6-52b4-a5fa-a4b1515a7b2d" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352180 1034 "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535497 true] [17592186352180 1111 #uuid "cfbc66d3-b386-518b-bd8b-28c4021e30d5" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352182 13194139535497 true] [17592186352181 1089 "q-124287" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535497 true] [17592186352181 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066119/assets/69a10b5524670d31cfc9f2982e37d58a5ee9ca39fe49f35b9dace011275a635a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(9-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along three-quarters of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(1,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352181 1113 #uuid "26a386e8-4f71-5301-b2d4-aba31281f90a" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352181 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352183 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352184 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352185 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352186 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352187 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1041 "selection" 13194139535497 true] [17592186352182 1104 #edb/ref 1759218635